-- Patch: corrects the DELETE wording in the verbs lesson of Digital Foundations.
-- Updates the lesson text and one quiz question in place, so progress and
-- comments are kept. Safe to run more than once.
UPDATE pm_learning_lessons SET content = '## Verbs for working with things\n\nAn API treats everything as a **resource** (a repair, a tenant, a contractor), each with its own address. The **HTTP method** is the verb that says what you want to do with it. Together they give you the four basic operations on any data, often called **CRUD**: Create, Read, Update, Delete.\n\n![GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE with repairs examples](local:http-methods.svg)\n\n## GET: read\n\n"Show me something." It must **never change anything**. You can call it as many times as you like safely. Loading a page, searching, fetching a repair: all GETs.\n\n`GET /repairs/4821` returns repair 4821.\n\n## POST: create\n\n"Make a new one." The details go in the body. Each call typically creates another new thing, so sending it twice can make a **duplicate**: two repairs, two charges. This is why "do not double-click the Pay button" exists.\n\n`POST /repairs` with a body describing the leak creates repair 4822.\n\n## PUT: replace the whole thing\n\n"Here is the complete record. Swap it in." You send **every** field. Anything you leave out is treated as empty. PUT is **idempotent**, a long word for a simple idea: doing it again changes nothing more. Sending the same PUT ten times leaves things exactly as sending it once.\n\n`PUT /repairs/4821` with a full record replaces the stored one.\n\n## PATCH: change just part of it\n\n"Change only these fields." You send **only what is changing**, and everything else stays as it is.\n\n`PATCH /repairs/4821` with `{"priority": "emergency"}` changes the priority and nothing else.\n\n## DELETE: remove\n\n"Get rid of it." `DELETE /repairs/4821` asks the system to **remove** the repair, and in a real system that usually means it is **permanently erased from the database**. It is not the same as cancelling a job, archiving it or unpublishing it, which all keep the record and just change its status or visibility. Like PUT, repeating it ends up in the same state (it is gone).\n\nBecause DELETE is so final, well-designed systems often do not use it for things like repairs. To "cancel" a job they use **PATCH** to set the status to `cancelled`, and to "archive" one they set an `archived` flag. The record stays, so the history is still there for audit, finance and complaints. This is sometimes called a **soft delete**, and a true removal is a **hard delete**.\n\n## PUT or PATCH? The difference that costs real money\n\nImagine repair 4821 has an address, a description, a priority, an appointment time and a contractor. You only want to change the priority.\n\n- With **PATCH**, you send `{"priority": "emergency"}`. Everything else stays.\n- With **PUT**, you must send the **entire** record. If a careless system sends only `{"priority": "emergency"}` as a PUT, the address, appointment and contractor may be **wiped out**.\n\nThis is a classic cause of mysterious data loss in integrations. When a supplier says their API supports "updates", ask: **is that PUT or PATCH, and what happens to fields I do not send?**\n\nWatch the same request play out both ways:\n\n![PUT replaces the whole record so missing fields are blanked, PATCH changes only the field sent](local:put-vs-patch.svg)\n\n## The less common two\n\n- **HEAD**: like GET, but returns only the headers (is it there? when was it last changed?). Efficient for checking.\n- **OPTIONS**: asks the server "what methods are allowed here?" Browsers use it behind the scenes for security checks.\n\n## Is it safe to repeat? A quick summary\n\nDevelopers use the word **idempotent** for an action that gives the same result however many times you do it. Pressing "set the light to on" is idempotent: press it five times and the light is simply on. A toggle button is not: press it five times and the light ends up in a different state. This matters because networks fail and requests get re-sent, so you need to know which actions are safe to repeat.\n\n- **GET** reads. Safe to repeat as often as you like.\n- **POST** creates. **Not** safe to repeat: it may make duplicates.\n- **PUT** replaces. Safe to repeat: the end result is the same.\n- **PATCH** partly updates. Usually safe to repeat, depending on how it is designed.\n- **DELETE** removes. Safe to repeat: once it is gone, deleting again leaves it gone.\n\n::: repairs\n- **GET** a list of today\'s jobs for a contractor\'s app.\n- **POST** a new repair raised by a tenant through the portal.\n- **PATCH** the status of a job to "in progress" when the operative arrives.\n- **PUT** a replacement contractor profile after a full review.\n- **DELETE** a duplicate booking created by mistake.\n\nNotice how many real repairs problems (duplicates, lost fields, wrong status) map neatly to a method being used wrongly.\n:::\n\n::: tip\nNot every API follows these conventions strictly. Some use POST for everything. A well-documented API says clearly which method does what.\n:::'
WHERE title = 'The HTTP methods: GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE';

UPDATE pm_learning_quiz_questions SET prompt = 'Why does a real API often set a status such as "cancelled" or "archived" instead of truly deleting a repair record?'
WHERE prompt = 'Why does a real API often \"cancel\" or \"archive\" instead of truly deleting a repair record?';
